# The seam between "what a target depends on" and "where it comes from".
#
# Every third-party dependency is named here as an alias, and ordinary targets
# depend on the alias -- //third_party:doctest -- never on the @nixpkgs_doctest
# repository behind it. That indirection costs one line per dependency and buys
# the ability to change the provider without touching a single BUILD file in
# modules/.
#
# --- why this matters beyond tidiness ---------------------------------------
#
# rules_nixpkgs works by shelling out to `nix-build` while Bazel is fetching
# repositories. That is fine on a developer machine and in CI, and it is wrong
# in exactly one important place: inside a nixpkgs derivation. A nixpkgs build
# runs in a sandbox with no network and no access to the Nix daemon, so a build
# that invokes Nix recursively cannot work there -- which means a project wired
# directly to @nixpkgs_* repositories can never be packaged *in* nixpkgs.
#
# The escape is to keep every such reference behind these aliases. Packaging
# this project for nixpkgs then means pointing the aliases at cc_library
# targets that describe the headers and libraries Nix has already placed in the
# sandbox via buildInputs -- a change confined to this package. Nothing under
# modules/ knows which of the two it got.
#
# That switch is not implemented yet; there is one provider today. The point is
# that adding the second one is a local change, and it stops being one the
# moment a BUILD file elsewhere spells out @nixpkgs_doctest.

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

# The unit-test framework. Header-only as far as consumers are concerned: the
# library that doctest ships holds a default main(), which this project does
# not use -- every runner defines DOCTEST_CONFIG_IMPLEMENT and supplies its own.
alias(
    name = "doctest",
    actual = "@nixpkgs_doctest//:doctest",
)
